{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794477715,"nonce":"UFhpbsyyCtqfbbYNgokzs7VOYhk=","message":"Producer landed locally in fb9905877d5fa82fd05d95601f77bb617a6803ee and 185f6b120976f3575b56b4e6bcf29314e925b92e (not pushed). Off by default; BlincConfig::node_table or BLINC_NODE_TABLE=1 turns it on.\n\nWhat it does: with the table on, motion-bound subtrees are painted as vectors into the dynamic batch once. Each primitive names the row of its innermost binding (parent chain to four, found from the layout tree where ranges are equal), and a frame in which only bound values change writes rows and leaves the batch alone. Group opacity stays a value on its layer command. Rows scale border widths and shadow geometry with the node, as a paint does.\n\nGate: the harness has a vector paint (a full paint every frame with no promotion) and a table path. Seven scenes (translate, scale, turn, fade, all four together, nested translate over scale and turn, overlapping fade) move on rows alone, every frame, and match the vector paint to one channel level. I saw the gate fail for real while building this: a clipped layer, unscaled borders, and rows applied about the wrong pivot inside an offscreen layer. The recorder suite (305, goldens included) and blinc_app (373) pass with the table off and with it forced on.\n\nFindings that change the picture\n- apply_binding_deltas is bypassed in practice. A dynamic batch that has been through cross-stream resolution carries the mark even with no captures, and the patcher rejects it, so a bound node is baked to a texture (promoted) and blitted. The patcher is close to dead code on real frames, so e238df7 and c321a49 are defects of the promoted path and the patcher fallback, not of the table.\n- The old reference was not the truth for these scenes: the promoted blit softens edges (fractional offsets resample the baked texture), so a vector paint differs from it by a ring of edge pixels, up to 72 levels for a translate. The table is held to the vector paint instead.\n- A row's pivot is in window space, so the offscreen layer passes (which shift primitives by the layer origin) and layer sizing (from primitive bounds) must see where a row puts a primitive. The renderer now does that on the CPU for layers and ordered groups; direct draws stay on the GPU.\n\nLimits, each declined to the old behaviour\n- A primitive with a clip in a bound range (the clip of the node's own should move with it, an ancestor's should not, and a row cannot tell): this will decline most real content in scroll viewports, and is the first thing to fix.\n- Text and SVG in a bound subtree are not moved by rows yet; those frames repaint fully as before. Glyph primitives with slots are next.\n- A layer with effects, blend or a 3D transform; a stretch with a turn; a turned ancestor; chains over four; CSS animations (apply_css_deltas is untouched).\n- With the table on, subtrees that a row cannot draw are no longer promoted, so they repaint fully where they used to blit a baked texture. Acceptable behind an off-by-default flag, not for turning it on.\n- meta.last_screen_aabb is not refreshed in table mode.\n- Not measured: no timings yet. The P0 benchmark must show the per-frame patch, upload and full-walk costs gone before this counts as done.","files":null}]}